supports an impossibility result.\n  - requested_fix: Remove the impossibility sentence, or state it as general background without attributing it to these citations. Describe the cited works only as addressing conditional-coverage guarantees, as their titles indicate.\n30\n73: author = {Tiffany Ding and Anastasios Nikolas Angelopoulos and Stephen Bates and Michael I. Jordan and R. Tibshirani},\n197:@Book{Vovk2005Algorithmic,\n199: author = {Vladimir Vovk and Alex Gammerman and Glenn Shafer},\n207: author = {Jing Lei and Max G'Sell and Alessandro Rinaldo and Ryan J. Tibshirani and Larry Wasserman},\n227:@Inproceedings{Romano2020Classification,\n229: author = {Yaniv Romano and Matteo Sesia and Emmanuel J. Cand{\\`e}s},\n244:@Inproceedings{Tibshirani2019Conformal,\n246: author = {Ryan J. Tibshirani and Rina Foygel Barber and Emmanuel J. Cand{\\`e}s and Aaditya Ramdas},\n253:@Article{Aitchison1982Statistical,\n255: author = {John Aitchison},\n65:Exact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has been proposed for bias reduction \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-conditional coverage with many classes \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP}, conformal prediction for long-tailed classification \\citep{Ding2025ConformalPF}, and class-wise coverage via augmented label rank calibration \\citep{Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n116:The procedure is built around three expectations, each tied to one component. \\textbf{H1}: grouping calibration by label (small $K$) or confidence bin (large $K$) should shrink worst-slice under-coverage relative to pooled calibration, because it makes the coverage guarantee hold within the evaluator's slices when those coincide with the groups. \\textbf{H2}: a gated, cross-fitted density-ratio correction should reduce marginal under-coverage on shifted cases, while a gate that stays closed leaves exchangeable behaviour and the negative controls untouched. \\textbf{H3}: group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds could further reduce worst-slice gaps when the groups are overlapping. The retained evidence addresses these only partially: H1 is not isolated (no pooled-versus-Mondrian pair of otherwise identical configurations was retained, and baseline identities are unknown); H2 is addressed by the development comparison V1 versus V2 and by the sealed shift-case gap; H3 is untestable from V3 because V3 coincides with V2. The aim of the sealed evaluation is thus the narrower statement of Section~\\ref{sec:results} about the composite score, not a verification of each hypothesis.\n217:The synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design; the class-wise baseline shares this alignment, but its individual score is not retained. The development score was used to choose the configuration, so development numbers may be optimistic; this was not measured, and the cause of the development-to-sealed drop is not isolated. The sealed evaluation used data disjoint from development by content hash; we have no retained record that it was run only once, and we could not verify which of the two tied configurations (V2 or V3) the sealed metrics belong to beyond their ranking position, so the frozen configuration's identity is not independently verified. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9bb6fb7c-aff9-42d5-acbb-24ba1a90eff1","uuid":"5a42c466-bfce-4a9e-ae28-b959ffdfaeef","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:31:15.839Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T10:31:04.584545+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/r02/claude/20261111_085834/paper/paper_evidence_audit.json`\n- audit_markdown_path: `work/results/r02/claude/20261111_085834/paper/paper_evidence_audit.md`\n- raw_output_path: `work/results/r02/claude/20261111_085834/paper/paper_evidence_audit_raw.txt`\n\n## Summary\nHeadline numbers, derived quantities, figures and implementation constants match the evidence trail, but three visible statements go beyond what the retained evidence supports.\n\n## Findings\n- `slice-axis-advantage-1` (Discussion and Limitations (Threats to validity)): The sentence asserts that the procedure has an advantage on the slice axis. The paper itself says per-axis baseline values are not retained and that the composite margin does not show dominance on each axis, so no slice-axis advantage over any baseline is measured. The implied attribution to method-metric alignment is also not tested.\n  - claim: so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design\n  - evidence: paper.tex Discussion (What the evidence supports says per-axis baseline values are not retained; What it does not support (ii) says only the best baseline score is retained); claims_grounding.json has no per-axis baseline claim; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics holds only aggregate best_baseline_score and margin; the evaluator's classwise baseline calibrates per label for every K.\n  - requested_fix: Rewrite to a conditional statement, for example: for K<=10 the evaluator slices coincide with the procedure's Mondrian groups, so slice-axis results may partly reflect this alignment; no per-axis baseline comparison is retained, so any slice-axis advantage is unmeasured.\n- `h2-sealed-gap-1` (Method (Design rationale and hypotheses)): The sealed shift-case gap (0.0536) is a single absolute value for the weighted procedure. No sealed unweighted counterpart or baseline shift gap is retained, so it cannot address whether the weighting reduced shift under-coverage. Only the development V1 versus V2 comparison (0.0613 to 0.0201, three seeds) bears on H2. The sealed gap is in fact larger than the development value.\n  - claim: H2 is addressed by the development comparison V1 versus V2 and by the sealed shift-case gap\n  - evidence: paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.shift_coverage_gap=0.0536 with no sealed V1 or baseline shift metric; derived/derived_stats.json variants V1 and V2 shift_coverage_gap; claims_grounding.json claim on development versus final shift gap says cause not isolated.\n  - requested_fix: Say H2 is supported only descriptively by the development V1 versus V2 comparison on three seeds. State that the sealed shift gap is reported as a residual and does not test H2.\n- `conditional-impossibility-1` (Related Work (Conditional validity)): The impossibility statement and its motivational link to the cited works have no claims_grounding.json entry and no retained evidence. The grounding describes these references by title and venue only. The titles ('Conformal prediction with conditional guarantees', 'Kandinsky Conformal Prediction: Beyond Class- and Covariate-Conditional Coverage') do not establish the claim, and the paper says elsewhere that such descriptions rest on titles and venues alone.\n  - claim: Exact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees (Gibbs2023, Bairaktari2025)\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json related-work claim for Gibbs2023ConformalPW and Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP is limited to 'titles and venues'; references_provenance.json holds only title, venue and year for both; no source id supports an impossibility result.\n  - requested_fix: Remove the impossibility sentence, or state it as general background without attributing it to these citations. Describe the cited works only as addressing conditional-coverage guarantees, as their titles indicate.\n30\n73: author = {Tiffany Ding and Anastasios Nikolas Angelopoulos and Stephen Bates and Michael I. Jordan and R. Tibshirani},\n197:@Book{Vovk2005Algorithmic,\n199: author = {Vladimir Vovk and Alex Gammerman and Glenn Shafer},\n207: author = {Jing Lei and Max G'Sell and Alessandro Rinaldo and Ryan J. Tibshirani and Larry Wasserman},\n227:@Inproceedings{Romano2020Classification,\n229: author = {Yaniv Romano and Matteo Sesia and Emmanuel J. Cand{\\`e}s},\n244:@Inproceedings{Tibshirani2019Conformal,\n246: author = {Ryan J. Tibshirani and Rina Foygel Barber and Emmanuel J. Cand{\\`e}s and Aaditya Ramdas},\n253:@Article{Aitchison1982Statistical,\n255: author = {John Aitchison},\n65:Exact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has been proposed for bias reduction \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-conditional coverage with many classes \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP}, conformal prediction for long-tailed classification \\citep{Ding2025ConformalPF}, and class-wise coverage via augmented label rank calibration \\citep{Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n116:The procedure is built around three expectations, each tied to one component. \\textbf{H1}: grouping calibration by label (small $K$) or confidence bin (large $K$) should shrink worst-slice under-coverage relative to pooled calibration, because it makes the coverage guarantee hold within the evaluator's slices when those coincide with the groups. \\textbf{H2}: a gated, cross-fitted density-ratio correction should reduce marginal under-coverage on shifted cases, while a gate that stays closed leaves exchangeable behaviour and the negative controls untouched. \\textbf{H3}: group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds could further reduce worst-slice gaps when the groups are overlapping. The retained evidence addresses these only partially: H1 is not isolated (no pooled-versus-Mondrian pair of otherwise identical configurations was retained, and baseline identities are unknown); H2 is addressed by the development comparison V1 versus V2 and by the sealed shift-case gap; H3 is untestable from V3 because V3 coincides with V2. The aim of the sealed evaluation is thus the narrower statement of Section~\\ref{sec:results} about the composite score, not a verification of each hypothesis.\n217:The synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design; the class-wise baseline shares this alignment, but its individual score is not retained. The development score was used to choose the configuration, so development numbers may be optimistic; this was not measured, and the cause of the development-to-sealed drop is not isolated. The sealed evaluation used data disjoint from development by content hash; we have no retained record that it was run only once, and we could not verify which of the two tied configurations (V2 or V3) the sealed metrics belong to beyond their ranking position, so the frozen configuration's identity is not independently verified. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
