{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HhLVqyaLwcH89jDr1bTYw2","type":"tool_result","content":"152:        \"Gibbs2023ConformalPW\",\n156:        \"Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP\",\n39:\\section{Introduction}\n47:\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n60:\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n61:\\paragraph{Foundations and classification scores.}\n64:\\paragraph{Conditional validity.}\n67:\\paragraph{Efficiency and the classifier.}\n70:\\paragraph{Other output types and settings.}\n73:\\paragraph{Distribution shift.}\n76:\\paragraph{Positioning.}\n79:\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n80:\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\\label{sec:setup}\n84:\\subsection{Composite reliability score}\n93:\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\n99:\\subsection{Statistical background and what it predicts}\n100:\\paragraph{Marginal and Mondrian validity.}\n103:\\paragraph{Proposition (Mondrian coverage; textbook).}\n108:\\paragraph{Weighted calibration under covariate shift.}\n111:\\paragraph{Why a one-sided loss favours a small margin.}\n115:\\subsection{Design rationale and hypotheses}\n118:\\subsection{Studied procedure}\n119:\\paragraph{Mondrian LAC.}\n123:\\paragraph{Gated cross-fitted weighting.}\n127:\\subsection{Compared configurations}\n131:\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n132:\\paragraph{Development data.} V1--V3 are scored with seeds 101--103 on the evaluator's development cases (per the declared reset sequence, three seeds on each of four case designs, plus the three controls); all three configurations produced valid runs and none was unscored. The development scores of V2 and V3 tie exactly. We attribute the sealed evaluation to V2 on the basis of its position in the development ranking (tied with V3); the selection manifest referenced by the evaluation record is not retained in the workspace, so this identity is not independently verified.\n133:\\paragraph{Sealed final data.} The frozen procedure is evaluated on sealed cases with seeds 9001--9005, disjoint from the development data by content hash. Each seeded run's score is the mean over cases of $1-\\ell$; the reported final score is the equal-weight mean over seeds and cases. Development and final numbers use different cases and seeds and are never pooled.\n134:\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} We report the five per-seed final scores, their sample standard deviation, and the evaluator's bootstrap interval for the paired margin. For the three development seeds we report paired per-seed differences without a significance test: with three pairs, the smallest attainable two-sided exact sign-flip $p$-value is $0.25$.\n136:\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n137:\\subsection{Sealed final comparison}\n173:\\subsection{Reliability profile and residual failures}\n186:\\subsection{Development-time comparison of nested configurations}\n199:\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\n202:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n203:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, since per-axis baseline values are not retained, and a different weighting could change the ranking. The interval half-width is about 0.011 and the margin is roughly twice the per-seed standard deviation of the procedure's scores (0.019), but baseline per-seed scores are not retained, so a seed-level paired effect size cannot be computed.\n205:\\paragraph{What it does not support.}\n214:\\paragraph{Evidence inventory.} Table~\\ref{tab:inventory} (Supplementary Information) separates available from unknown quantities, so gaps are not mistaken for null results.\n216:\\paragraph{Threats to validity.}\n219:\\paragraph{What would change the conclusion.} The positive margin would be weakened if per-baseline results showed that the best baseline wins on the slice or size axis and loses only on a few shifted cases, or if the sealed cases were mostly shifted. It would be strengthened by per-case margins whose intervals all exclude zero, by gate logs showing the gate closed on exchangeable cases, and by replication on cases generated from different families.\n221:\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every method (Figure~\\ref{fig:decomp}).\n223:\\section{Conclusion}\n234:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n236:\\subsection{Procedure specification}\n262:\\paragraph{Pseudo-code.}\n266:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\n270:\\subsection{Full development and per-seed tables}\n323:\\subsection{Derived quantities}\n331:\\subsection{Reproducibility and what is not retained}\n351:\\subsection{LLM Usage}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9bb6fb7c-aff9-42d5-acbb-24ba1a90eff1","uuid":"150a0243-4302-4b1e-9256-7b189aa22fed","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:31:21.101Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"152:        \"Gibbs2023ConformalPW\",\n156:        \"Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP\",\n39:\\section{Introduction}\n47:\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n60:\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n61:\\paragraph{Foundations and classification scores.}\n64:\\paragraph{Conditional validity.}\n67:\\paragraph{Efficiency and the classifier.}\n70:\\paragraph{Other output types and settings.}\n73:\\paragraph{Distribution shift.}\n76:\\paragraph{Positioning.}\n79:\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n80:\\subsection{Problem setup and evaluation target}\\label{sec:setup}\n84:\\subsection{Composite reliability score}\n93:\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\n99:\\subsection{Statistical background and what it predicts}\n100:\\paragraph{Marginal and Mondrian validity.}\n103:\\paragraph{Proposition (Mondrian coverage; textbook).}\n108:\\paragraph{Weighted calibration under covariate shift.}\n111:\\paragraph{Why a one-sided loss favours a small margin.}\n115:\\subsection{Design rationale and hypotheses}\n118:\\subsection{Studied procedure}\n119:\\paragraph{Mondrian LAC.}\n123:\\paragraph{Gated cross-fitted weighting.}\n127:\\subsection{Compared configurations}\n131:\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n132:\\paragraph{Development data.} V1--V3 are scored with seeds 101--103 on the evaluator's development cases (per the declared reset sequence, three seeds on each of four case designs, plus the three controls); all three configurations produced valid runs and none was unscored. The development scores of V2 and V3 tie exactly. We attribute the sealed evaluation to V2 on the basis of its position in the development ranking (tied with V3); the selection manifest referenced by the evaluation record is not retained in the workspace, so this identity is not independently verified.\n133:\\paragraph{Sealed final data.} The frozen procedure is evaluated on sealed cases with seeds 9001--9005, disjoint from the development data by content hash. Each seeded run's score is the mean over cases of $1-\\ell$; the reported final score is the equal-weight mean over seeds and cases. Development and final numbers use different cases and seeds and are never pooled.\n134:\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} We report the five per-seed final scores, their sample standard deviation, and the evaluator's bootstrap interval for the paired margin. For the three development seeds we report paired per-seed differences without a significance test: with three pairs, the smallest attainable two-sided exact sign-flip $p$-value is $0.25$.\n136:\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n137:\\subsection{Sealed final comparison}\n173:\\subsection{Reliability profile and residual failures}\n186:\\subsection{Development-time comparison of nested configurations}\n199:\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\n202:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n203:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, since per-axis baseline values are not retained, and a different weighting could change the ranking. The interval half-width is about 0.011 and the margin is roughly twice the per-seed standard deviation of the procedure's scores (0.019), but baseline per-seed scores are not retained, so a seed-level paired effect size cannot be computed.\n205:\\paragraph{What it does not support.}\n214:\\paragraph{Evidence inventory.} Table~\\ref{tab:inventory} (Supplementary Information) separates available from unknown quantities, so gaps are not mistaken for null results.\n216:\\paragraph{Threats to validity.}\n219:\\paragraph{What would change the conclusion.} The positive margin would be weakened if per-baseline results showed that the best baseline wins on the slice or size axis and loses only on a few shifted cases, or if the sealed cases were mostly shifted. It would be strengthened by per-case margins whose intervals all exclude zero, by gate logs showing the gate closed on exchangeable cases, and by replication on cases generated from different families.\n221:\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every method (Figure~\\ref{fig:decomp}).\n223:\\section{Conclusion}\n234:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n236:\\subsection{Procedure specification}\n262:\\paragraph{Pseudo-code.}\n266:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\n270:\\subsection{Full development and per-seed tables}\n323:\\subsection{Derived quantities}\n331:\\subsection{Reproducibility and what is not retained}\n351:\\subsection{LLM Usage}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvzC5A2Gp6nRfA1KSWG7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ARdry38EWPyj5o8qrVCS1v","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 145,165p claims_grounding.json; ls scripts; sed -n 60,78p paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2488,"cache_read_input_tokens":47336,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2488},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9bb6fb7c-aff9-42d5-acbb-24ba1a90eff1","uuid":"307ddb47-56f8-4603-924e-ce25c5b9e2d9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:31:23.267Z","request_id":"req_011CgvzC4anjpCY6qyPTkzNN"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ARdry38EWPyj5o8qrVCS1v","type":"tool_result","content":"        \"ref.s2.5d074986e32ab5c912c05ed48f87cdd892024cb2\",\n        \"ref.s2.45c540d0ed7ab1809ec4feb5d892b32250e224be\",\n        \"ref.s2.84e3461b3fd473c7fabb6f008085a99fe85b560d\",\n        \"ref.s2.c275d0da3df4b0e1250f4c66a68990820d79c800\",\n        \"ref.s2.ef26ca9f4c4086efbb87e640150b721877528875\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\n        \"Gibbs2023ConformalPW\",\n        \"Ding2023ClassConditionalCP\",\n        \"Shi2024ConformalPF\",\n        \"Melki2023GroupConditionalCP\",\n        \"Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP\",\n        \"Kaur2025ConformalPS\",\n        \"Jin2024ConfidenceOT\",\n        \"Löfström2015BiasRT\",\n        \"Ding2025ConformalPF\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Related work: efficiency, classifier adaptation, temperature scaling, foundation-model features, Monte Carlo and interpretable-network conformal methods (titles/venues only).\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\nbuild_figures.py\nderive_stats.py\n\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n\\paragraph{Foundations and classification scores.}\nSplit conformal prediction uses a held-out calibration set and retains finite-sample marginal coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic,Lei2018Distribution}; Mondrian conformal prediction applies the same rank argument within partition cells \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic}. For classifiers, the least-ambiguous-set construction thresholds the softmax mass of each label \\citep{Sadinle2019Least}, adaptive prediction sets accumulate sorted probability mass with randomisation \\citep{Romano2020Classification}, and a regularised variant penalises large ranks to shrink sets on many-class problems \\citep{Angelopoulos2020Uncertainty}. These are our comparators (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n\n\\paragraph{Conditional validity.}\nSeveral works address conditional-coverage guarantees beyond the marginal guarantee, including conditional guarantees for conformal prediction \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW} and coverage beyond class- and covariate-conditional notions \\citep{Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has been proposed for bias reduction \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-conditional coverage with many classes \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP}, conformal prediction for long-tailed classification \\citep{Ding2025ConformalPF}, and class-wise coverage via augmented label rank calibration \\citep{Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n\n\\paragraph{Efficiency and the classifier.}\nSet size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Conformal methods have also been combined with Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n\n\\paragraph{Other output types and settings.}\nConformal methods have been developed for time series \\citep{Xu2022ConformalPS}, ordinal labels \\citep{Chakraborty2024DistributionfreeCP}, heteroskedastic regression with conditional validity \\citep{Dewolf2023ConditionalVO}, multivariate residuals \\citep{Braun2025MultivariateSR}, epistemic-uncertainty settings \\citep{Javanmardi2025OptimalCP} and connections to scenario optimisation \\citep{O'Sullivan2025BridgingCP}. Applied treatments of conformal classifiers appear in clinical imaging and histopathology \\citep{Cina2026QuantifyingCC,Gelvez2025ConformalPF}. We describe these works only by title and venue.\n\n\\paragraph{Distribution shift.}\nUnder covariate shift, exchangeability fails. If the density ratio is known, reweighting the calibration scores by it and giving the test point its own weight restores marginal coverage \\citep{Tibshirani2019Conformal}; estimating the ratio with a calibration-versus-test classifier makes the guarantee approximate. Our gated step follows this recipe with an explicit unweighted fallback.\n\n\\paragraph{Positioning.}\nOur contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry \\citep{Aitchison1982Statistical} motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9bb6fb7c-aff9-42d5-acbb-24ba1a90eff1","uuid":"6ca00bac-2735-4469-9e6c-049ae6971a6e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:31:23.344Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"        \"ref.s2.5d074986e32ab5c912c05ed48f87cdd892024cb2\",\n        \"ref.s2.45c540d0ed7ab1809ec4feb5d892b32250e224be\",\n        \"ref.s2.84e3461b3fd473c7fabb6f008085a99fe85b560d\",\n        \"ref.s2.c275d0da3df4b0e1250f4c66a68990820d79c800\",\n        \"ref.s2.ef26ca9f4c4086efbb87e640150b721877528875\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\n        \"Gibbs2023ConformalPW\",\n        \"Ding2023ClassConditionalCP\",\n        \"Shi2024C