{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvzCziQn7H15cCLrBPyM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XJD4VTedVWnv74mn3odEEL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; export TMPDIR=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}; pip install -q --target $TMPDIR/pylib pypdf 2>&1 | tail -1; PYTHONPATH=$TMPDIR/pylib python3 -c \"\nimport pypdf\nd=pypdf.PdfReader('paper.pdf')\nfor i in (9,10): print('=====',i+1); print(d.pages[i].extract_text()[:3000])\n\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":267,"cache_read_input_tokens":52579,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":267},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9bb6fb7c-aff9-42d5-acbb-24ba1a90eff1","uuid":"310a7508-a0a8-424b-af29-6c96f51376f7","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:31:36.645Z","request_id":"req_011CgvzCz7h3rQGP6D3meM4N"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XJD4VTedVWnv74mn3odEEL","type":"tool_result","content":"[notice] To update, run: pip install --upgrade pip\n===== 10\nGenerated by The Agentic Scientist\n486\n487\n488\n489\n490\n491\n492\n493\n494\n495\n496\n497\n498\n499\n500\n501\n502\n503\n504\n505\n506\n507\n508\n509\n510\n511\n512\n513\n514\n515\n516\n517\n518\n519\n520\n521\n522\n523\n524\n525\n526\n527\n528\n529\n530\n531\n532\n533\n534\n535\n536\n537\n538\n539\nPractical reading. For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional out-\nputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous\nbehaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift\nand slice gaps seen here. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every\nmethod (Figure 3).\n6 C ONCLUSION\nWe studied a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-\nclassifier reweighting step under a multi-axis reliability protocol on synthetic softmax families. On\nsealed final data it reached a composite score of 0.8416 versus 0.8029 for the best of four standard\nbaselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95% interval of 0.0274–0.0499, with no failed\nepisodes and all negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not\nremove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposi-\ntion implies, by subtraction under the evaluator’s aggregation, that the marginal term is the largest\ncomponent (the marginal term is not measured directly, and α and per-case results are unavailable).\nThe conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline\ncomparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-\nbaseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping,\ngate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done\nhere.\n10\n===== 11\nGenerated by The Agentic Scientist\n540\n541\n542\n543\n544\n545\n546\n547\n548\n549\n550\n551\n552\n553\n554\n555\n556\n557\n558\n559\n560\n561\n562\n563\n564\n565\n566\n567\n568\n569\n570\n571\n572\n573\n574\n575\n576\n577\n578\n579\n580\n581\n582\n583\n584\n585\n586\n587\n588\n589\n590\n591\n592\n593\nREFERENCES\nJohn Aitchison. The statistical analysis of compositional data. Journal of the Royal Statistical Soci-\nety: Series B (Methodological), 44(2):139–160, 1982. doi: 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1982.tb01195.x.\nAnastasios N. Angelopoulos, Stephen Bates, Jitendra Malik, and Michael I. Jordan. Uncertainty sets\nfor image classifiers using conformal prediction, 2020.\nKonstantina Bairaktari, Jia-Yun Wu, and Steven Z. Wu. Kandinsky conformal prediction: Beyond\nclass- and covariate-conditional coverage. ArXiv, abs/2502.17264, 2025.\nDaniel Bethell, Simos Gerasimou, and R. Calinescu. Robust uncertainty quantification using con-\nformalised monte carlo prediction. ArXiv, abs/2308.09647, 2023.\nSacha Braun, E. Berta, Michael I. Jordan, and Francis R. Bach. Multivariate standardized residuals\nfor conformal prediction. 2025.\nS. Chakraborty, Chhavi Tyagi, Haiyan Qiao, and Wen-Ge Guo. Distribution-free conformal predic-\ntion for ordinal classification. pp. 120–139, 2024.\nAndrea Cina, Maria Monzon, Fabio Galbusera, and C. Jutzeler. Quantifying central canal stenosis\nprediction uncertainty in spinenet with conformal prediction. Scientific Reports, 16, 2026.\nLahav Dabah and Tom Tirer. On temperature scaling and conformal prediction of deep classifiers.\n2024.\nNicolas Dewolf, B. de Baets, and W. Waegeman. Conditional validity of heteroskedastic conformal\nregression. 2023.\nTiffany Ding, Anastasios Nikolas Angelopoulos, Stephen Bates, Michael I. Jordan, and R. Tibshi-\nrani. Class-conditional conformal prediction with many classes. ArXiv, abs/2306.09335, 2023.\nTiffany Ding, Jean-Baptiste Fermanian, and Joseph Salmon. Conformal prediction for long-tailed\nclassification. ArXiv, abs/2507.06867, 2025.\nLeo Fillioux, Julio Silva-Rodr’iguez, I. Ben Ayed, P. Courn `ede, M. Vakalopoulou,\nS. Christodoulidis, and J. Dolz. Are foundation models for computer vision good conformal\npredictors? ArXiv, abs/2412.06082, 2024.\nSantiago Gelvez, D. Clavijo, and David Romo-Bucheli. Conformal prediction for deep learning clas-\nsification model in histopathological images. 2025 XXV Symposium of Image, Signal Processing,\nand Artificial Vision (STSIVA), pp. 1–5, 2025.\nIsaac Gibbs, John J. Cherian, and E. Cand `es. Conformal prediction with conditional guarantees.\nJournal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B: Statistical Methodology, 2023.\nLinhui Huang, S. Lala, and N. Jha. Confine: Conformal prediction for interpretable neural networks.\nArXiv, abs/2406.00539, 2024.\nA. Javanmardi, Soroush H. Zargarbashi, Santo M. A. R. Thies, Willem Waegeman, Aleksandar\nBojchevski, and Eyke H ¨ullermeier. Optimal conformal prediction under epistemic uncertainty.\nArXiv, abs/2505.19033, 2025.\nYing Jin and Zhi-Mei Ren. Confidence on the focal: Conformal prediction with","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9bb6fb7c-aff9-42d5-acbb-24ba1a90eff1","uuid":"0be1f937-b6db-4e39-bcbb-606556ae6c86","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:31:38.397Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"[notice] To update, run: pip install --upgrade pip\n===== 10\nGenerated by The Agentic Scientist\n486\n487\n488\n489\n490\n491\n492\n493\n494\n495\n496\n497\n498\n499\n500\n501\n502\n503\n504\n505\n506\n507\n508\n509\n510\n511\n512\n513\n514\n515\n516\n517\n518\n519\n520\n521\n522\n523\n524\n525\n526\n527\n528\n529\n530\n531\n532\n533\n534\n535\n536\n537\n538\n539\nPractical reading. For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional out-\nputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous\nbehaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift\nand slice gaps seen here. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every\nmethod (Figure 3).\n6 C ONCLUSION\nWe studied a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-\nclassifier reweighting step under a multi-axis reliability protocol on synthetic softmax families. On\nsealed final data it reached a composite score of 0.8416 versus 0.8029 for the best of four standard\nbaselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95% interval of 0.0274–0.0499, with no failed\nepisodes and all negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not\nremove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposi-\ntion implies, by subtraction under the evaluator’s aggregation, that the marginal term is the largest\ncomponent (the marginal term is not measured directly, and α and per-case results are unavailable).\nThe conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline\ncomparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-\nbaseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping,\ngate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done\nhere.\n10\n===== 11\nGenerated by The Agentic Scientist\n540\n541\n542\n543\n544\n545\n546\n547\n548\n549\n550\n551\n552\n553\n554\n555\n556\n557\n558\n559\n560\n561\n562\n563\n564\n565\n566\n567\n568\n569\n570\n571\n572\n573\n574\n575\n576\n577\n578\n579\n580\n581\n582\n583\n584\n585\n586\n587\n588\n589\n590\n591\n592\n593\nREFERENCES\nJohn Aitchison. The statistical analysis of compositional data. Journal of the Royal Statistical Soci-\nety: Series B (Methodological), 44(2):139–160, 1982. doi: 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1982.tb01195.x.\nAnastasios N. Angelopoulos, Stephen Bates, Jitendra Malik, and Michael I. Jordan. Uncertainty sets\nfor image classifiers using conformal prediction, 2020.\nKonstantina Bairaktari, Jia-Yun Wu, and Steven Z. Wu. Kandinsky conformal prediction: Beyond\nclass- and covariate-conditional coverage. ArXiv, abs/2502.17264, 2025.\nDaniel Bethell, Simos Gerasimou, and R. Calinescu. Robust uncertainty quantification using con-\nformalised monte carlo prediction. ArXiv, abs/2308.09647, 2023.\nSacha Braun, E. Berta, Michael I. Jordan, and Francis R. Bach. Multivariate standardized residuals\nfor conformal prediction. 2025.\nS. Chakraborty, Chhavi Tyagi, Haiyan Qiao, and Wen-Ge Guo. Distribution-free conformal predic-\ntion for ordinal classification. pp. 120–139, 2024.\nAndrea Cina, Maria Monzon, Fabio Galbusera, and C. Jutzeler. Quantifying central canal stenosis\nprediction uncertainty in spinenet with conformal prediction. Scientific Reports, 16, 2026.\nLahav Dabah and Tom Tirer. On temperature scaling and conformal prediction of deep classifiers.\n2024.\nNicolas Dewolf, B. de Baets, and W. Waegeman. Conditional validity of heteroskedastic conformal\nregression. 2023.\nTiffany Ding, Anastasios Nikolas Angelopoulos, Stephen Bates, Michael I. Jordan, and R. Tibshi-\nrani. Class-conditional conformal prediction with many classes. ArXiv, abs/2306.09335, 2023.\nTiffany Ding, Jean-Baptiste Fermanian, and Joseph Salmon. Conformal prediction for long-tailed\nclassification. ArXiv, abs/2507.06867, 2025.\nLeo Fillioux, Julio Silva-Rodr’iguez, I. Ben Ayed, P. Courn `ede, M. Vakalopoulou,\nS. Christodoulidis, and J. Dolz. Are foundation models for computer vision good conformal\npredictors? ArXiv, abs/2412.06082, 2024.\nSantiago Gelvez, D. Clavijo, and David Romo-Bucheli. Conformal prediction for deep learning clas-\nsification model in histopathological images. 2025 XXV Symposium of Image, Signal Processing,\nand Artificial Vision (STSIVA), pp. 1–5, 2025.\nIsaac Gibbs, John J. Cherian, and E.