{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y6oHe5dPuFRew5vcjcd9sz","type":"tool_result","content":"\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n\\subsection{Sealed final comparison}\nThe studied procedure attains a sealed final score of 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best baseline (Table~\\ref{tab:final}, Figure~\\ref{fig:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.best_baseline_score]]\nThe paired margin is 0.0387 with bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499, which excludes zero. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.score_margin_vs_best_baseline]]\nIn loss terms the mean loss falls from 0.1971 to 0.1584, a relative reduction of 19.6\\%. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\nThe five seeded runs score between 0.812 and 0.862 (mean 0.8416, standard deviation 0.019), so every seeded run is above the aggregate best-baseline score, although this compares individual seeds with an aggregate. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_per_seed]]\nNo episode failed and all negative controls passed (fraction 1.0; count not retained), and the sealed qualification outcome is ``qualified'' with status ``completed''. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.qualification.outcome]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.controls_passed_fraction]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/final_comparison.pdf}\n\\caption{Sealed final comparison. (a) Paired score margin of the studied procedure over the best of four baselines with its 95\\% bootstrap interval (seeded runs within cases, equal weight over cases). (b) Per-seed final scores of the procedure; horizontal lines mark the procedure's aggregate score and the best-baseline aggregate score, so per-seed dots are compared with an aggregate.}\n\\label{fig:final}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final metrics retained by the evaluator. Gaps are one-sided shortfalls; ``shift'' is descriptive.}\n\\label{tab:final}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nComposite score of studied procedure & 0.8416 \\\\\nComposite score of best baseline (identity not retained) & 0.8029 \\\\\nPaired margin (95\\% bootstrap interval) & 0.0387 (0.0274, 0.0499) \\\\\nExchangeable-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0017 \\\\\nWorst-slice under-coverage gap (all cases) & 0.0675 \\\\\nShift-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0536 \\\\\nMean set-size ratio & 0.209 \\\\\nNegative controls passed (fraction; count not retained) & 1.0 \\\\\nFailed episodes & 0 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Reliability profile and residual failures}\nThe headline margin should be read together with the residual gaps. On exchangeable cases the mean marginal under-coverage gap is 0.0017, compatible with the finite-sample guarantee; because the gap is one-sided and averaged over a few seeded runs, this is not a test of the guarantee and does not show coverage is tight. The mean set-size ratio is 0.209. The shift-case marginal gap is 0.0536 and the worst-slice gap is 0.0675, so both residual failures are larger than the exchangeable gap by more than an order of magnitude. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.shift_coverage_gap]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.worst_slice_coverage_gap]]\n\nBecause Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss} is linear in the case-mean metrics, the mean loss of 0.1584 decomposes as follows: the set-size term contributes $0.25\\times0.209=0.0522$, the slice term $0.25\\times0.0675=0.0169$, and the remainder, $0.0893$, is the marginal term, equivalent to a mean clipped marginal gap of $0.179$ in units of $\\alpha$ over all cases. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\nThis subtraction relies on the evaluator aggregating the components exactly as reported, and $\\alpha$ is not retained, so we do not convert it to a coverage number. Since exchangeable-case gaps are small, the marginal term is plausibly dominated by shifted cases, but we cannot establish this without per-case results. By this subtraction the implied largest residual cost is marginal under-coverage rather than set size, subject to the caveats above. We do not attribute the residual shift or slice gaps to statistical noise, to the density-ratio estimation, or to a validity failure of the weighting: no gate-level diagnostics are retained.\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.78\\textwidth]{figures/loss_decomposition.pdf}\n\\caption{Decomposition of the sealed final mean loss of the studied procedure into the three terms of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss}. The set-size and worst-slice terms are computed from the retained metrics; the marginal term is the remainder. The best baseline's total loss is shown without decomposition because its components are not retained.}\n\\label{fig:decomp}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Development-time comparison of nested configurations}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:variants} shows the three development configurations. Adding the gated weighting to Mondrian LAC (V1 to V2) raised the development score from 0.8683 to 0.9192, reduced the shift-case gap from 0.0613 to 0.0201 and the worst-slice gap from 0.0497 to 0.0376, and left the exchangeable marginal gap unchanged at 0.00028; set size grew from 0.166 to 0.181. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\nThe per-seed paired differences in score are 0.064, 0.043 and 0.045, all positive, but with three seeds this is descriptive rather than inferential. Because the components are changed together we can say only that the V2 configuration outperformed V1 on these development cases, not which element of the weighting step is responsible.\n\nV3 reproduced V2 in every retained field: score, all six metrics and all three per-seed scores. The V3 source falls back to the V2 path verbatim when its calibration-only gate does not activate, which would give this outcome; but whether the gate was inactive or the quantile-regression thresholds were exercised and rejected is not recorded, so the cause of the tie remains unknown. We therefore report no evidence for or against quantile-regression thresholds.\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/variant_profile.pdf}\n\\caption{Development-time comparison of three nested configurations (V1: Mondrian LAC; V2: plus gated weighting, the studied procedure; V3: plus quantile-regression thresholds). Panels show shift-case marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio (lower is better) and per-seed scores over seeds 101--103 (higher is better; bar is the mean). V3 coincides with V2 in every retained value. Development cases and seeds differ from the sealed final data.}\n\\label{fig:variants}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\nThe development score of the selected procedure (0.9192) is higher than its sealed final score (0.8416), and the final shift gap (0.0536) and set-size ratio (0.209) exceed the development values (0.0201, 0.181). The two evaluations use different seeds and case sets, and sealed cases may be harder; the retained artifacts do not isolate the cause, so we make no statement about selection effects or over-fitting. Only the sealed numbers should be used for conclusions; Table~\\ref{tab:devfinal} in the Supplementary Information lists both sets side by side.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, since per-axis baseline values are not retained, and a different weighting could change the ranking. The interval half-width is about 0.011 and the margin is roughly twice the per-seed standard deviation of the procedure's scores (0.019), but baseline per-seed scores are not retained, so a seed-level paired effect size cannot be computed.\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.}\n(i) \\emph{Real data.} No real-data run exists; behaviour on classifier outputs from natural datasets is untested.\n(ii) \\emph{Baseline identity.} Only the best baseline score is retained; we cannot say which baseline it is or how the others compare.\n(iii) \\emph{Cases and families.} Per-case and per-family results, $\\alpha$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases are unknown; the bootstrap interval does not cover variation across case designs.\n(iv) \\emph{Mechanism.} The comparison among V1--V3 is a design comparison on three seeds and does not isolate the contribution of any single component of the weighting step; V3 provides no information on quantile regression.\n(v) \\emph{Validity versus statistics.} The residual shift and slice gaps are reported as measured; the diagnostics needed to separate estimation error in the density ratio, small-sample noise and violated assumptions are not available, and candidate-side diagnostics are unverified.\n(vi) \\emph{Range.} The tested range is limited by the evaluator ($K\\le100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le2000$); nothing is claimed about larger $K$, very small calibration sets, or simplex-valued regression targets.\n(vii) \\emph{Metric.} One-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage except through set size, and the Mondrian guarantee is conditional on label or confidence bin, not on arbitrary slices, so slice gaps in other partitions are not controlled by theory.\n\n\\paragraph{Evidence inventory.} Table~\\ref{tab:inventory} (Supplementary Information) separates available from unknown quantities, so gaps are not mistaken for null results.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.}\nThe synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so slice-axis results may partly reflect this alignment between method and metric; the class-wise baseline calibrates per label as well, and no per-axis baseline comparison is retained, so any slice-axis advantage is unmeasured. The development score was used to choose the configuration, so development numbers may be optimistic; this was not measured, and the cause of the development-to-sealed drop is not isolated. The sealed evaluation used data disjoint from development by content hash; we have no retained record that it was run only once, and we could not verify which of the two tied configurations (V2 or V3) the sealed metrics belong to beyond their ranking position, so the frozen configuration's identity is not independently verified. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.\n\n\\paragraph{What would change the conclusion.} The positive margin would be weakened if per-baseline results showed that the best baseline wins on the slice or size axis and loses only on a few shifted cases, or if the sealed cases were mostly shifted. It would be strengthened by per-case margins whose intervals all exclude zero, by gate logs showing the gate closed on exchangeable cases, and by replication on cases generated from different families.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every method (Figure~\\ref{fig:decomp}).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9bb6fb7c-aff9-42d5-acbb-24ba1a90eff1","uuid":"4852ce8c-87ab-42cd-b71a-183f24274d6a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:31:44.071Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n\\subsection{Sealed final comparison}\nThe studied procedure attains a sealed final score of 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best baseline (Table~\\ref{tab:final}, Figure~\\ref{fig:final}). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.best_baseline_score]]\nThe paired margin is 0.0387 with bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499, which excludes zero. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.score_margin_vs_best_baseline]]\nIn loss terms the mean loss falls from 0.1971 to 0.1584, a relative reduction of 19.6\\%. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\nThe five seeded runs score between 0.812 and 0.862 (mean 0.8416, standard deviation 0.019), so every seeded run is above the aggregate best-baseline score, although this compares individual seeds with an aggregate. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_per_seed]]\nNo episode failed and all negative controls passed (fraction 1.0; count not retained), and the sealed qualification outcome is ``qualified'' with status ``completed''. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.qualification.outcome]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.controls_passed_fraction]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth]{figures/final_comparison.pdf}\n\\caption{Sealed final comparison. (a) Paired score margin of the studied procedure over the best of four baselines with its 95\\% bootstrap interval (seeded runs within cases, equal weight over cases). (b) Per-seed final scores of the procedure; horizontal lines mark the procedure's aggregate score and the best-baseline aggregate score, so per-seed dots are compared with an aggregate.}\n\\label{fig:final}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final metrics retained by the evaluator. Gaps are one-sided shortfalls; ``shift'' is descriptive.}\n\\label{tab:final}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nComposite score of studied procedure & 0.8416 \\\\\nComposite score of best baseline (identity not retained) & 0.8029 \\\\\nPaired margin (95\\% bootstrap interval) & 0.0387 (0.0274, 0.0499) \\\\\nExchangeable-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0017 \\\\\nWorst-slice under-coverage gap (all cases) & 0.0675 \\\\\nShift-case marginal under-coverage gap & 0.0536 \\\\\nMean set-size ratio & 0.209 \\\\\nNegative controls passed (fraction; count not retained) & 1.0 \\\\\nFailed episodes & 0 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Reliability profile and residual failures}\nThe headline margin should be read together with the residual gaps. On exchangeable cases the mean marginal under-coverage gap is 0.0017, compatible with the finite-sample guarantee; because the gap is one-sided and averaged over a few seeded runs, this is not a test of the guarantee and does not show coverage is tight. The mean set-size ratio is 0.209. The shift-case marginal gap is 0.0536 and the worst-slice gap is 0.0675, so both residual failures are larger than the exchangeable gap by more than an order of magnitude. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.shift_coverage_gap]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.worst_slice_coverage_gap]]\n\nBecause Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss} is linear in the case-mean metrics, the mean loss of 0.1584 decomposes as follows: the set-size term contributes $0.25\\times0.209=0.0522$, the slice term $0.25\\times0.0675=0.0169$, and the remainder, $0.0893$, is the marginal term, equivalent to a mean clipped marginal gap of $0.179$ in units of $\\alpha$ over all cases. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#final_loss_decomposition]]\nThis subtraction relies on the evaluator aggregating the components exactly as reported, and $\\alpha$ is not retained, so we do not convert it to a coverage number. Since exchangeable-case gaps are small, the marginal term is plausibly dominated by shifted cases, but we cannot establish this without per-case results. By this subtraction the implied largest residual cost is marginal under-coverage rather than set size, subject to the caveats above. We do not attribute the residual shift or slice gaps to statistical noise, to the density-ratio estimation, or to a validity failure of the weighting: no gate-level diagnostics are retained.\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.78\\textwidth]{figures/loss_decomposition.pdf}\n\\caption{Decomposition of the sealed final mean loss of the studied procedure into the three terms of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:loss}. The set-size and worst-slice terms are computed from the retained metrics; the marginal term is the remainder. The best baseline's total loss is shown without decomposition because its components are not retained.}\n\\label{fig:decomp}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Development-time comparison of nested configurations}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:variants} shows the three development configurations. Adding the gated weighting to Mondrian LAC (V1 to V2) raised the development score from 0.8683 to 0.9192, reduced the shift-case gap from 0.0613 to 0.0201 and the worst-slice gap from 0.0497 to 0.0376, and left the exchangeable marginal gap unchanged at 0.00028; set size grew from 0.166 to 0.181. % @evidence[[derived/derived_stats.json#variants]]\nThe per-seed paired differences in score are 0.064, 0.043 and 0.045, all positive, but with three seeds this is descriptive rather than inferential. Because the components are changed together we can say only that the V2 configuration outperformed V1 on these development cases, not which element of the weighting step is responsible.\n\nV3 reproduced V2 in every retained field: score, all six metrics and all three per-seed scores. The V3 source falls back to the V2 path verbatim when its calibration-only gate does not activate, which would give this outcome; but whether the gate was inactive or the quantile-regression thresholds were exercised and rejected is not recorded, so the cause of the tie remains unknown. We therefore report no evidence for or against quantile-regression thresholds.\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/variant_profile.pdf}\n\\caption{Development-time comparison of three nested configurations (V1: Mondrian LAC; V2: plus gated weighting, the studied procedure; V3: plus quantile-regression thresholds). Panels show shift-case marginal gap, worst-slice gap, set-size ratio (lower is better) and per-seed scores over seeds 101--103 (higher is better; bar is the mean). V3 coincides with V2 in every retained value. Development cases and seeds differ from the sealed final data.}\n\\label{fig:variants}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Development-to-final difference}\nThe development score of the selected procedure (0.9192) is higher than its sealed final score (0.8416), and the final shift gap (0.0536) and set-size ratio (0.209) exceed the development values (0.0201, 0.181). The two evaluations use different seeds and case sets, and sealed cases may be harder; the retained artifacts do not isolate the cause, so we make no statement about selection effects or over-fitting. Only the sealed numbers should be used for conclusions; Table~\\ref{tab:devfinal} in the Supplementary Information lists both sets side by side.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, since per-axis baseline values are not retained, and a different weighting could change the ranking. The interval half-width is about 0.011 and the margin is roughly twice the per-seed standard deviation of the procedure's scores (0.019), but baseline per-seed scores are not retained, so a seed-level paired effect size cannot be computed.\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.}\n(i) \\emph{Real data.} No real-data run exists; behaviour on classifier outputs from natural datasets is untested.\n(ii) \\emph{Baseline identity.} Only the best baseline score is retained; we cannot say which baseline it is or how the others compare.\n(iii) \\emph{Cases and families.} Per-case and per-family results, $\\alpha$ and sample sizes of the sealed cases are unknown; the bootstrap interval does not cover variation across case designs.\n(iv) \\emph{Mechanism.} The comparison among V1--V3 is a design comparison on three seeds and does not isolate the contribution of any single component of the weighting step; V3 provides no information on quantile regression.\n(v) \\emph{Validity versus statistics.} The residual shift and slice gaps are reported as measured; the diagnostics needed to separate estimation error in the density ratio, small-sample noise and violated assumptions are not available, and candidate-side diagnostics are unverified.\n(vi) \\emph{Range.} The tested range is limited by the evaluator ($K\\le100$, $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}\\le2000$); nothing is claimed about larger $K$, very small calibration sets, or simplex-valued regression targets.\n(vii) \\emph{Metric.} One-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage except through set size, and the Mondrian guarantee is conditional on label or confidence bin, not on arbitrary slices, so slice gaps in other partitions are not controlled by theory.\n\n\\paragraph{Evidence inventory.} Table~\\ref{tab:inventory} (Supplementary Information) separates available from unknown quantities, so gaps are not mistaken for null results.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.}\nThe synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so slice-axis results may partly reflect this alignment between method and metric; the class-wise baseline calibrates per label as well, and no per-axis baseline comparison is retained, so any slice-axis advantage is unmeasured. The development score was used to choose the configuration, so development numbers may be optimistic; this was not measured, and the cause of the development-to-sealed drop is not isolated. The sealed evaluation used data disjoint from development by content hash; we have no retained record that it was run only once, and we could not verify which of the two tied configurations (V2 or V3) the sealed metrics belong to beyond their ranking position, so the frozen configuration's identity is not independently verified. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.\n\n\\paragraph{What would change the conclusion.} The positive margin would be weakened if per-baseline results showed that the best baseline wins on the slice or size axis and loses only on a few shifted cases, or if the sealed cases were mostly shifted. It would be strengthened by per-case margins whose intervals all exclude zero, by gate logs showing the gate closed on exchangeable cases, and by replication on cases generated from different families.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every method (Figure~\\ref{fig:decomp}).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
