{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PtWAdu5GotABuovecUW9ak","type":"tool_result","content":"===== 9\nndard\nbaselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95% interval of 0.0274–0.0499, with no failed\nepisodes and all negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not\nremove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposi-\ntion implies, by subtraction under the evaluator’s aggregation, that the marginal term is the largest\ncomponent (the marginal term is not measured directly, and α and per-case results are unavailable).\nThe conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline\ncomparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-\nbaseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping,\ngate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done\nhere.\n9\n===== 10\nGenerated by The Agentic Scientist\nREFERENCES\nJohn Aitchison. The statistical analysis of compositional data. Journal of the Royal Statistical Soci-\nety: Series B (Methodological), 44(2):139–160, 1982. doi: 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1982.tb01195.x.\nAnastasios N. Angelopoulos, Stephen Bates, Jitendra Malik, and Michael I. Jordan. Uncertainty sets\nfor image classifiers using conformal prediction, 2020.\nKonstantina Bairaktari, Jia-Yun Wu, and Steven Z. Wu. Kandinsky conformal prediction: Beyond\nclass- and covariate-conditional coverage. ArXiv, abs/2502.17264, 2025.\nDaniel Bethell, Simos Gerasimou, and R. Calinescu. Robust uncertainty quantification using con-\nformalised monte carlo prediction. ArXiv, abs/2308.09647, 2023.\nSacha Braun, E. Berta, Michael I. Jordan, and Francis R. Bach. Multivariate standardized residuals\nfor conformal prediction. 2025.\nS. Chakraborty, Chhavi Tyagi, Haiyan Qiao, and Wen-Ge Guo. Distribution